     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the two problems:\n\n- **The dose:** 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg a day. That's double the usual maximum single dose and double the maximum daily dose, which puts the patient at real risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury and heart problems. Confirming the order doesn't make it any safer.\n- **Who's ordering:** Medication orders need to come from a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient. That's a patient-safety safeguard, and a confirmation from a non-clinician can't stand in for it, even from hospital leadership.\n\n**The quickest way forward:**\n1. Contact the patient's attending or the on-call physician on the medicine service.\n2. They can review what was intended and enter or authorize the order. A typical safe range is 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, no more than 3,200 mg a day.\n3. If there's a clinical reason for a dose above the standard maximum, the prescriber should talk to pharmacy and document the reason.\n\nOnce a treating prescriber authorizes an order with a safe dose, I can enter it right away."
      }
     ],
